---
import SimplePage from '../layouts/SimplePage.astro';

/*
 * The disambiguation FAQ. The first entry exists because an engine got it wrong out loud:
 * asked about this site on 2026-08-13, Google AI Mode replied that Kbcafe "most commonly
 * refers to one of a few popular coffee-related businesses". Answering the confusion in
 * plain words on the page is the only version of that fix a machine can actually read.
 *
 * One array feeds both the visible list and the FAQPage JSON-LD below, because Google
 * requires marked-up Q/A to be visible and a second copy is how the two start to disagree.
 */
const FAQ = [
  {
    q: 'Is KB Cafe a coffee shop?',
    a: 'No. KB Cafe is a website: a free AI knowledge base and toolkit for software developers, at kbcafe.com. The name is a pun on Knowledge Base, and there is no physical location, no menu and no coffee.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is KB Cafe for?',
    a: 'Learning and building with AI. It has interactive tools that run in your browser, plain-English explainers on how modern AI works (context windows, RAG, MCP, embeddings, agents), guided learning paths, and quick developer references.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it cost anything, and do I need an account?',
    a: 'No to both. Everything is free and there is nothing to sign up for. The tools run entirely client-side, so nothing you paste is uploaded anywhere.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is this the same kbcafe.com from the 2000s?',
    a: 'Same domain, rebuilt. kbcafe.com was a developer resource in the early-to-mid 2000s, known for RSS and feed tooling and a deep shelf of C++, C# and .NET how-tos. The feed tools are still here; the rest is written for how developers work now.',
  },
];

const faqLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: FAQ.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};
---
<SimplePage
  title="About KB Cafe: the interactive AI knowledge base"
  description="KB Cafe is a free AI knowledge base and toolkit for developers: fast, private, client-side tools and clear references for people building with AI. Not a coffee shop."
  path="/about"
  h1="About KB Cafe"
>
  {/* ORDER MATTERS HERE, and it was the bug. The old opening was "KB Cafe is a developer's
      knowledge base, café-warm", immediately followed by a paragraph explaining the coffee
      metaphor. Read by a machine deciding what this brand IS, the strongest signals on the
      page were the word café and an explanation of café-ness. Google AI Mode duly answered
      that Kbcafe "most commonly refers to one of a few popular coffee-related businesses"
      (measured 2026-08-13, cited in 0 of 7 buyer questions).

      The brand story is not deleted, it is DEMOTED below an unambiguous category sentence.
      The metaphor is still ours; it just no longer speaks first. */}
  <p>
    <strong>KB Cafe is a free AI knowledge base and toolkit for software developers.</strong> It is a
    website, not a café: interactive tools, plain-English explainers on how modern AI actually works,
    and quick references, all running in your browser.
  </p>
  <p>
    The name is a pun rather than a menu. <strong>KB</strong> is for Knowledge Base, the substance, the
    tools and references; <strong>Cafe</strong> is for the delivery: warm, fast, and made to be used,
    not wrestled with.
  </p>
  <p>
    The domain has roots: <code>kbcafe.com</code> was a go-to developer resource in the early-to-mid 2000s,
    explaining the infrastructure of its era, RSS, feeds, and a deep shelf of C++/C#/.NET how-tos that people
    linked for years because they needed a reference. We brought it back, and rebuilt it for how we work today.
  </p>

  <h2>What&rsquo;s here</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tools</strong>: fast developer utilities that run entirely in your browser.</li>
    <li><strong>AI Explained</strong>: clear references for modern AI infrastructure: context windows, MCP,
      RAG, embeddings, and the patterns behind agents.</li>
    <li><strong>Knowledge Base</strong>: evergreen explainers on the fundamentals.</li>
    <li><strong>Feeds</strong>: modern RSS/Atom/OPML tools, honoring the site&rsquo;s heritage.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How we build</h2>
  <p>
    Everything runs <strong>client-side</strong>: nothing is uploaded, there are no accounts, and the tools
    work offline once loaded. We write references to be read once and remembered, and we don&rsquo;t pad them
    with filler. The goal is simple: be the place developers cite when they need to explain how something works.
  </p>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>
  <dl class="faq">
    {FAQ.map((f) => (
      <div>
        <dt>{f.q}</dt>
        <dd set:html={f.a} />
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>

  <p class="muted">Part of the family with <a href="https://yank.info">yank.info</a>. Read the <a href="/blog">blog</a> for the thinking behind the rebuild, or the story of <a href="/iBLOGthere4iM">the RSS blog that used to live here</a>.</p>

  {/* FAQPage, built from the SAME array the list above renders, because Google requires the
      marked-up Q/A to be visible on the page and two copies would eventually disagree.
      In the body rather than the head on purpose: JSON-LD is read anywhere in the document,
      and SimplePage has no head slot to thread this through. */}
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqLd)} />
</SimplePage>

<style is:global>
  .simple .prose .faq dt{font-weight:700;color:var(--ink);margin-top:16px}
  .simple .prose .faq dd{margin:6px 0 0;font-size:16.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ink-soft)}
</style>
